22:00:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I see exactly what you’re getting at — it feels like I’m dancing around the obvious fact that both involve *heat*, so heat must be the real cause. That’s a completely fair reduction. Let me test that idea directly with a couple of simple experiments, and I think you’ll see where the "extra steps" become essential, not evasive. --- ### Experiment 1: Same Temperature, Different Medium Take two identical cotton T-shirts. - **Shirt A:** Submerge it in **100°C (212°F) boiling water** for 10 minutes with no agitation at all. It will shrink significantly. - **Shirt B:** Place it in a **100°C convection oven** (same temperature as the water) for 10 minutes with no moisture. It will not shrink at all. Both were at the exact same temperature. If heat alone were the root cause, both would shrink equally. But they don’t. So heat *alone* can’t be the trigger — something about the water is essential. ### Experiment 2: Lower Temperature, With the "Other Factors" Now take two wool swatches. - **Swatch A:** Put it in **cold water** and agitate it vigorously by hand. It will felt and shrink over time. - **Swatch B:** Press it with a dry iron at **150°C** with zero moisture and absolutely no sideways movement (just direct vertical pressure, no sliding). It will not shrink. Here, the cold wash causes shrinkage while a much hotter iron does not. That makes it impossible to blame heat as the root cause — the cold process does what the hot process fails to do. --- ### Why This Shows Heat Is Necessary But Not Sufficient I’m not denying heat plays a role; it’s a powerful accelerator. But in physics and chemistry, there’s a difference between a **cause** and an **enabler**. Shrinkage is a physical rearrangement: fibers must slide past each other into a more compact arrangement. For that sliding to happen, they need to be **liberated from their current locked state**. The thing that unlocks them is water, not heat. - In cotton and linen, water molecules break the hydrogen bonds that hold fibers in shape. Without water, those bonds stay locked no matter how hot the dry fibers get (until they burn). Heat alone from an iron can’t break enough of those bonds to let fibers slide — it just makes the fibers vibrate faster but they remain in position. - In wool, water lubricates the scales so they can slide and catch. A dry iron’s heat might soften the fiber slightly, but without moisture, the scales don’t glide; they resist. With even a tiny bit of steam or natural moisture, suddenly they can move. You could think of it like this: heat is the fuel that makes a car go, but the car still needs an engine and transmission. Without those, pouring gasoline into an empty frame won’t make it move. Water is the engine. Agitation steers it. Heat just speeds up the process. ### The Iron Seems Hotter, Yet Doesn’t Shrink — Why? Because an iron on dry cotton (the standard case where it’s safe) delivers heat without the one thing that makes fibers mobile: water. The fibers are rigid even at 200°C because the hydrogen bonds are intact. The moment you add moisture (like steam-ironing cotton) you’re re-introducing the plasticizer, but now you’re holding the fabric taut, so you force it into the shape you want rather than letting it collapse. Without that taut hold, steam ironing can indeed shrink cotton slightly, which is why tailors use steam to "shrink out" fullness — they purposefully relax the fabric without restraint. For wool, the scales mean that even a slight slide with moisture tangles them, so steam ironing wool is the perfect felting recipe — exactly as your grandma knew. That’s entirely consistent with the principle: moisture + friction, with heat speeding it all up. ### So, Is It Really About Heat? No. Heat is just the most obvious part of the story because we associate "hot" with damage, but the chain of causation is: **water breaks bonds → fibers become mobile → agitation forces them to compact → heat accelerates the whole thing.** Remove water, and the chain stops, regardless of heat. Remove agitation (for non-wool), and you can have hot water with minimal shrinkage. I’m not avoiding saying heat is the cause; I’m detailing the **mechanism** because if you just say “heat causes shrinking,” you’d be wrong in a way that leads to confusing contradictions (like why a hot iron and a hot dryer behave differently). The root cause is moisture-driven mobility, and heat is its loud sidekick. That’s why the generational advice about wool is specifically about *pressing with steam* (or sliding), not just heat from a lamp bulb.
